<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      This source manifest declares no permissions. AndroidX may add its own
      package-signature permission to the merged manifest; it is not a sensitive
      runtime permission and does not grant network access.

      RotorLens needs no INTERNET permission because it never contacts a network:
      the viewer is served from the APK and the log is decoded on the device. An
      app that cannot reach the internet cannot upload somebody's flight, and the
      absence of this line is the strongest form that promise can take.
    -->

    <!--
      Nothing leaves the device, including to another device the owner owns.

      android:allowBackup="false" covers cloud backup, and on releases before
      Android 12 it covered device-to-device transfer as well. It no longer does:
      for apps targeting API 31+, Android's Auto Backup documentation states that
      on devices from some manufacturers allowBackup="false" "doesn't disable
      device-to-device transfers for the app", and that with no <device-transfer>
      section "all the application data will be transferred during a D2D
      migration". targetSdk is 36, so this app is squarely in that case.

      That mattered the moment the flight history started living in
      getFilesDir()/flight-history.json: setting up a new phone from this one
      would have carried it over, and the store listing, the in-app disclaimer and
      the privacy policy all say "Nothing leaves your phone". The rules file
      excludes everything from both cloud backup and device transfer.
    -->

    <!--
      USB host, so a flight controller in mass-storage mode can offer to open
      RotorLens and save the pilot a step. android:required="false" because
      nothing else in this app needs USB, and a phone without host support must
      still be able to install it.

      THIS IS A FEATURE, NOT A PERMISSION, and that distinction is exactly the
      kind of technically-true that lets a safety claim rot, so it is written
      down rather than left to be discovered. Declaring the USB_DEVICE_ATTACHED
      filter below grants this app permission to COMMUNICATE with the device that
      launched it. RotorLens never does, and never should: the log is still
      chosen by the pilot through the system picker, exactly as before.

      Before this line, "RotorLens never connects to or writes to a flight
      controller" was true because no USB code existed in the binary. It is still
      true for the same reason, and test/provenance.test.mjs now asserts the
      absence rather than trusting it. The day a UsbManager, a UsbDeviceConnection
      or an MSP command encoder appears, that test fails — and the store listing
      and the in-app disclaimer have to change with it.
    -->
    <uses-feature
        android:name="android.hardware.usb.host"
        android:required="false" />

    <application
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.RotorLens">

        <!--
          configChanges: every change listed here is handled by MainActivity
          instead of recreating it. Recreation destroys the WebView and runs
          onDestroy, which unlinks the open log, so a pilot who opened a log from
          the picker and then rotated, entered split screen, unfolded the phone,
          resized a desktop window, or changed display size, font size or
          language came back to an empty viewer.

          The WebView re-lays itself out for size and density. Font size it does
          not follow on its own, so MainActivity.onConfigurationChanged re-applies
          it. uiMode (dark mode, car/desk dock) needs nothing: the theme has no
          night variant and the page is dark-only; test/android-shell.test.mjs
          fails if either of those stops being true.

          Still recreated, deliberately left out: mcc|mnc (SIM change),
          touchscreen, colorMode, fontWeightAdjustment (bold text) and
          grammaticalGender. None is something a pilot does mid-analysis, and
          whether the WebView follows them in place has not been checked.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|density|fontScale|layoutDirection|locale|keyboard|keyboardHidden|navigation|uiMode"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
              Opening a log from the mass-storage drive or a file manager. Blackbox
              logs have no registered MIME type and arrive as octet-stream or
              text/plain depending on the provider, so the extension is matched.

              WHY EACH EXTENSION APPEARS FIVE TIMES. A pathPattern's ".*" does not
              backtrack: it stops at the FIRST occurrence of the character after
              it and commits. So ".*\\.bbl" only matched a path whose first dot
              was the extension's, and refused LOG00012.bak.bbl, flight.2026.bbl,
              and anything in a folder like "Rotorflight 4.6" — Uri.getPath() is
              decoded, so a document provider's folder names are part of it.

              pathSuffix (Android 12+) matches the real end of the path whatever
              comes before it. Android 8-11 ignore that attribute, so for them the
              patterns spell out one to four dots; a path with five or more still
              misses there and can be shared or picked instead.
              test/android-shell.test.mjs pins both, against a model of the
              platform matcher that was checked against its source.
            -->
            <intent-filter android:label="@string/open_with">
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <data android:scheme="content" />
                <data android:scheme="file" />
                <data android:mimeType="*/*" />
                <data android:host="*" />
                <data android:pathSuffix=".bbl" tools:targetApi="31" />
                <data android:pathSuffix=".BBL" tools:targetApi="31" />
                <data android:pathSuffix=".bfl" tools:targetApi="31" />
                <data android:pathSuffix=".BFL" tools:targetApi="31" />
                <data android:pathSuffix=".txt" tools:targetApi="31" />
                <data android:pathSuffix=".TXT" tools:targetApi="31" />
                <data android:pathSuffix=".log" tools:targetApi="31" />
                <data android:pathSuffix=".LOG" tools:targetApi="31" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\.bbl" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\.bbl" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\.bbl" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\..*\\.bbl" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\.BBL" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\.BBL" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\.BBL" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\..*\\.BBL" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\.bfl" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\.bfl" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\.bfl" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\..*\\.bfl" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\.BFL" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\.BFL" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\.BFL" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\..*\\.BFL" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\.txt" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\.txt" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\.txt" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\..*\\.txt" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\.TXT" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\.TXT" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\.TXT" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\..*\\.TXT" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\.log" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\.log" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\.log" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\..*\\.log" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\.LOG" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\.LOG" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\.LOG" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\..*\\.LOG" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
              The same match for a VIEW intent that carries no MIME type.

              A filter declaring any mimeType matches ONLY intents that have one,
              so the filter above cannot see the typeless intents many file
              managers send for an extension the system has no mapping for — and
              .bbl has none. A filter with no mimeType matches only typeless
              intents, so the two are complementary rather than overlapping.

              android:host="*" is required, not decoration: IntentFilter nests the
              path test inside the authority branch, so with no host the
              pathPatterns below would never be evaluated at all.

              The path rules are the same as the filter above, for the same reason.
            -->
            <intent-filter android:label="@string/open_with">
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <data android:scheme="content" />
                <data android:scheme="file" />
                <data android:host="*" />
                <data android:pathSuffix=".bbl" tools:targetApi="31" />
                <data android:pathSuffix=".BBL" tools:targetApi="31" />
                <data android:pathSuffix=".bfl" tools:targetApi="31" />
                <data android:pathSuffix=".BFL" tools:targetApi="31" />
                <data android:pathSuffix=".txt" tools:targetApi="31" />
                <data android:pathSuffix=".TXT" tools:targetApi="31" />
                <data android:pathSuffix=".log" tools:targetApi="31" />
                <data android:pathSuffix=".LOG" tools:targetApi="31" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\.bbl" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\.bbl" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\.bbl" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\..*\\.bbl" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\.BBL" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\.BBL" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\.BBL" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\..*\\.BBL" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\.bfl" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\.bfl" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\.bfl" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\..*\\.bfl" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\.BFL" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\.BFL" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\.BFL" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\..*\\.BFL" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\.txt" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\.txt" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\.txt" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\..*\\.txt" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\.TXT" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\.TXT" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\.TXT" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\..*\\.TXT" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\.log" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\.log" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\.log" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\..*\\.log" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\.LOG" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\.LOG" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\.LOG" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\..*\\..*\\.LOG" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!-- Sharing a log into RotorLens from any app. -->
            <intent-filter android:label="@string/share_to">
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:mimeType="application/octet-stream" />
                <data android:mimeType="text/plain" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
              A flight controller entering mass-storage mode offers to open here.

              The sequence today is: connect the board, open the Rotorflight
              configurator, tap the mass storage button, leave that app, find
              RotorLens, open it, pick the file. This removes one of those steps —
              Android offers RotorLens the moment the board mounts, and the "always"
              checkbox makes it automatic from then on.

              Matched by device CLASS rather than vendor ID; see
              res/xml/usb_mass_storage.xml for why. It fires for USB storage
              generally, which is deliberate: a card reader is the import path for
              pilots whose controller logs to a removable card rather than to
              onboard dataflash, and both of the boards this was measured against
              are dataflash-only.

              This intent carries a UsbDevice and grants permission to talk to it.
              RotorLens reads neither: handleIntent has no branch for
              ACTION_USB_DEVICE_ATTACHED, so the activity simply comes to the
              foreground and the pilot picks the log exactly as before.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.hardware.usb.action.USB_DEVICE_ATTACHED" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.hardware.usb.action.USB_DEVICE_ATTACHED"
                android:resource="@xml/usb_mass_storage" />
        </activity>
    </application>
</manifest>
